      "claim": "Valbenazine, the closest structural analog with a tetrabenazine-like core and a basic amine side chain, has an absolute oral bioavailability of approximately 49% in humans, which is clearly above the 20% threshold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C46"],
      "claim": "Donepezil, a dimethoxy-substituted CNS drug with a basic amine, has essentially complete oral bioavailability (100%), supporting high oral absorption for this structural class.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C33"],
      "claim": "Urapidil, a dimethoxyphenyl piperazine derivative, has an absolute oral bioavailability of about 78%, indicating that dimethoxyphenyl-containing basic drugs can achieve high systemic exposure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C63"],
      "claim": "Gallopamil, a verapamil analog with additional methoxy substitution, shows very high oral bioavailability (96.3%), supporting high bioavailability for highly methoxylated basic molecules.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C38"],
      "claim": "Tetrabenazine, a related benzoquinolizine without the basic amine side chain, has very low oral bioavailability (~5%), but this is due to extensive first-pass metabolism that is not expected to apply to the query's more basic, less lipophilic structure.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is most closely related to valbenazine (F≈49%) and shares features with high-bioavailability dimethoxyphenyl basic drugs such as donepezil, urapidil, and gallopamil. Low-bioavailability analogs are structurally or mechanistically less transferable. Therefore, the query is predicted to have F ≥ 20%.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental bioavailability data for the query molecule itself.",
